{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Neural Network Weight.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Neural Network Weight.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"neural network weight neural network weight","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Neural Network Weight.md","fileName":"Neural Network Weight.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Neural Network Weight","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Artificial Neural Network Structure]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Artificial Neuron","Weighted Input","Loss Function","Decision Boundary","Layer Matrix Computation","algorithms.epub","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Neural Network Weight.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Neural Network Weight.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"neural network weight neural network weight","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Neural Network Weight.md","fileName":"Neural Network Weight.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Neural Network Weight","heading":"Neural Network Weight","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A neural-network weight is a numerical parameter applied to one input of an [[Artificial Neuron]]. Multiplying each input by its weight determines how strongly and in which direction that connection influences the neuron's [[Weighted Input]].\n\nTraining changes weights to reduce a [[Loss Function]]. A positive or negative value can make a connection excitatory or inhibitory in the simplified computational model, while the collection of learned weights stores much of the network's fitted behavior.\n\nKelleher interprets a neuron's input weights as a vector. For a threshold unit with two inputs, changing that vector rotates the [[Decision Boundary]] because the boundary is orthogonal to the weight direction. Weights can also be collected into a matrix for [[Layer Matrix Computation]], so all neurons' weighted sums in a fully connected layer are calculated together.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Artificial Neuron","Weighted Input","Loss Function","Decision Boundary","Layer Matrix Computation","algorithms.epub","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Neural Network Weight.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Neural Network Weight.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"neural network weight neural network weight","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Neural Network Weight.md","fileName":"Neural Network Weight.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Neural Network Weight","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[algorithms.epub]]\n\n[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Artificial Neuron","Weighted Input","Loss Function","Decision Boundary","Layer Matrix Computation","algorithms.epub","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}